[Ms. H] It seems to come and go.

471
00:18:34,780 --> 00:18:36,865
[Mr. X] His leg's suddenly flaring up.

472
00:18:36,866 --> 00:18:38,367
Yeah, it's feeling worse.

473
00:18:38,951 --> 00:18:39,952
The letter "I."

474
00:18:47,543 --> 00:18:49,294
OBJECT CLUES COLLECTED

475
00:18:49,295 --> 00:18:52,130
[Mr. P]
We'll never catch the killer at this rate.

476
00:18:52,131 --> 00:18:53,965
[♪ whimsical music playing]

477
00:18:53,966 --> 00:18:55,508
[Ms. H] What's this? Is it a clue?

478
00:18:55,509 --> 00:18:57,135
- That's nothing.
- No, it's not.

479
00:18:57,136 --> 00:18:58,928
- [Ms. H] Is it ours?
- That's the cam shooting us.

480
00:18:58,929 --> 00:19:00,513
- [players laughing]
- Oh, I thought it was evidence.

481
00:19:00,514 --> 00:19:02,892
- I was about to check the SD card.
- [Mr. P] No.

482
00:19:03,768 --> 00:19:05,227
- Just this, then?
- [Mr. T] There's more.

483
00:19:05,853 --> 00:19:07,562
It's just a production camera.

484
00:19:07,563 --> 00:19:09,814
- [Mr. X] So funny.
- [Ms. H] I'm too immersed in the game.

485
00:19:09,815 --> 00:19:11,191
My clue is a mobile phone.

486
00:19:11,192 --> 00:19:13,652
- [Mr. P] A mobile phone?
- Yeah. I wonder whose it is.

487
00:19:13,653 --> 00:19:14,736
Can you turn it on?

488
00:19:14,737 --> 00:19:15,904
[Mr. L] Look in the photo gallery.

489
00:19:15,905 --> 00:19:17,739
- [Mr. P] What?
- I just got chills.

490
00:19:17,740 --> 00:19:19,532
- Why?
- [Mr. T] Whose is it? Who's that?

491
00:19:19,533 --> 00:19:21,076
- [Mr. L] It's a family photo.
- Look.

492
00:19:21,077 --> 00:19:23,453
- This is…
- Mr. P?

493
00:19:23,454 --> 00:19:25,789
[Ms. H]
It seems to be a photo of Mr. P's family.

494
00:19:25,790 --> 00:19:27,792
These two are his family.

495
00:19:28,376 --> 00:19:31,127
I'm guessing this is
his wife and daughter.

496
00:19:31,128 --> 00:19:32,963
But the ages don't seem to add up.

497
00:19:33,798 --> 00:19:35,757
How old are you, Mr. P?

498
00:19:35,758 --> 00:19:36,925
I'm 35 years old.

499
00:19:36,926 --> 00:19:38,343
What are you saying? [chuckles]

500
00:19:38,344 --> 00:19:39,427
[Mr. P] I'm 35!

501
00:19:39,428 --> 00:19:41,596
I don't believe you
since you're a scammer.

502
00:19:41,597 --> 00:19:43,431
His job took a toll on him.
What can he do?

503
00:19:43,432 --> 00:19:46,059
If he's really 35,
then I think this is his wife.

504
00:19:46,060 --> 00:19:50,355
MR. P IS A FAMILY MAN
WITH A WIFE AND DAUGHTER?

505
00:19:50,356 --> 00:19:52,482
I found evidence dating back ten years

506
00:19:52,483 --> 00:19:55,610
about the adoption of a female minor.

507
00:19:55,611 --> 00:19:57,446
This is the admission record.

508
00:19:57,947 --> 00:19:59,949
"She was placed
in a child protection facility."

509
00:20:00,825 --> 00:20:01,908
Ten years ago.

510
00:20:01,909 --> 00:20:03,493
I think it's her.

511
00:20:03,494 --> 00:20:04,787
The adopted girl.

512
00:20:05,454 --> 00:20:06,831
Do you think so?

513
00:20:07,540 --> 00:20:09,165
I never adopted anyone.

514
00:20:09,166 --> 00:20:11,584
If it was ten years ago,
she might be the adoptee.

515
00:20:11,585 --> 00:20:12,877
CRACKING UP

516
00:20:12,878 --> 00:20:14,379
What is the family connection here?

517
00:20:14,380 --> 00:20:16,297
Are they his daughter and grandchild

518
00:20:16,298 --> 00:20:17,674
or his wife and daughter?

519
00:20:17,675 --> 00:20:20,094
Come on. Don't attack me about my looks.

520
00:20:20,636 --> 00:20:22,137
She is my daughter.

521
00:20:22,138 --> 00:20:24,389
I learned from the MASTER

522
00:20:24,390 --> 00:20:27,392
and joined the organization
to strike it rich.

523
00:20:27,393 --> 00:20:28,476
But I needed a caregiver.

524
00:20:28,477 --> 00:20:30,396
I put her into care so that I could work.

525
00:20:30,980 --> 00:20:36,735
HE ABANDONED HIS DAUGHTER
TO JOIN A CRIMINAL ORGANIZATION

526
00:20:36,736 --> 00:20:39,112
Is there any other evidence?

527
00:20:39,113 --> 00:20:41,156
- [Mr. P] What's that?
- "You're under arrest."

528
00:20:41,157 --> 00:20:43,700
- [Mr. P] We're no strangers to handcuffs.
- [Mr. X] We basically

529
00:20:43,701 --> 00:20:45,410
wear them like bracelets.

530
00:20:45,411 --> 00:20:48,913
Are they the ones that were used
to hang the MASTER

531
00:20:48,914 --> 00:20:50,039
when he died?

532
00:20:50,040 --> 00:20:51,958
Oh, there's blood on it.

533
00:20:51,959 --> 00:20:53,418
- [Mr. L] <i>So there is.</i>
- <i>If you look here…</i>

534
00:20:53,419 --> 00:20:54,794
BLOODSTAINED HANDCUFFS

535
00:20:54,795 --> 00:20:57,297
If he was dead, he should have collapsed,

536
00:20:57,298 --> 00:20:58,757
but he's hanging by those handcuffs.

537
00:20:58,758 --> 00:21:00,176
I find that really strange.

538
00:21:01,260 --> 00:21:03,928
Don't you think killing someone
and then hanging them up

539
00:21:03,929 --> 00:21:05,305
is a bit psycho?

540
00:21:05,306 --> 00:21:08,058
Of course.
A normal person wouldn't do this.

541
00:21:08,726 --> 00:21:10,186
Well, actually…

542
00:21:10,811 --> 00:21:12,646
- I hung him up.
- What?

543
00:21:13,230 --> 00:21:14,856
THE PSYCHO IS AMONG US

544
00:21:14,857 --> 00:21:16,232
[Mr. T, Mr. P, Mr. X laughing]

545
00:21:16,233 --> 00:21:17,358
TOTALLY UNFAZED

546
00:21:17,359 --> 00:21:18,360
- [Mr. X] Why?
- Well--

547
00:21:18,944 --> 00:21:22,113
THIS KIND OF BRAZEN CONFESSION IS A FIRST

548
00:21:22,114 --> 00:21:23,948
- I hung my brother up.
- [Mr. P] Why?

549
00:21:23,949 --> 00:21:25,450
Yes, I hung him up,

550
00:21:25,451 --> 00:21:28,953
but he was still alive
when I handcuffed him.

551
00:21:28,954 --> 00:21:31,247
We got into a fight

552
00:21:31,248 --> 00:21:33,166
so I had no choice but to restrain him.

553
00:21:33,167 --> 00:21:36,544
And then I left,
but when I came back, he was dead.

554
00:21:36,545 --> 00:21:38,547
Around what time did that happen?

555
00:21:39,131 --> 00:21:40,465
I think I handcuffed him

556
00:21:40,466 --> 00:21:43,051
<i>between 00:16 and 00:19.</i>

557
00:21:43,052 --> 00:21:45,512
<i>-The first…</i>
- [Mr. P] <i>You should know, you hung him up.</i>

558
00:21:45,513 --> 00:21:47,138
I tied him up and left,

559
00:21:47,139 --> 00:21:48,598
but when I came back, he was dead.

560
00:21:48,599 --> 00:21:49,682
- In that time?
- [Mr. L] Yes, so…

561
00:21:49,683 --> 00:21:50,809
Within a few minutes?

562
00:21:50,810 --> 00:21:52,853
I really want to find
the person who killed him.

563
00:21:53,604 --> 00:21:55,522
[chuckles]
Even though you got into a fight?

564
00:21:55,523 --> 00:21:58,817
So far, you are the most likely suspect.

565
00:21:58,818 --> 00:22:03,196
- Can you explain why you cuffed him?
- Yeah. We had a fight--

566
00:22:03,197 --> 00:22:06,115
That's no reason to handcuff him
and hang him from the ceiling.

567
00:22:06,116 --> 00:22:08,576
Were you mad he left you behind
when the car crashed?

568
00:22:08,577 --> 00:22:10,955
That was part of the reason.

569
00:22:11,622 --> 00:22:13,290
That's a strange reason.

570
00:22:13,999 --> 00:22:15,583
Why did he leave after cuffing him?

571
00:22:15,584 --> 00:22:19,504
They were alone, so he could have
easily cuffed him normally.

572
00:22:19,505 --> 00:22:21,673
And he has a bad leg,
so that'd be less trouble.

573
00:22:21,674 --> 00:22:23,550
It's common for accomplices to fight.

574
00:22:23,551 --> 00:22:25,176
They could have fought over profits.

575
00:22:25,177 --> 00:22:28,304
But I don't think Mr. L
would kill his brother over money.

576
00:22:28,305 --> 00:22:31,808
I think it would be a family issue,

577
00:22:31,809 --> 00:22:34,853
maybe some kind of betrayal.

578
00:22:34,854 --> 00:22:37,689
At this point, it's hard to say.

579
00:22:37,690 --> 00:22:39,566
- I brought evidence on myself.
- Mr. T.

580
00:22:39,567 --> 00:22:40,775
- Yes.
- A diary?

581
00:22:40,776 --> 00:22:42,862
- [Mr. P] <i>Are you confessing?</i>
- [Mr. T] <i>It's nothing like that.</i>

582
00:22:43,362 --> 00:22:45,530
Business was good back then.

583
00:22:45,531 --> 00:22:48,366
[Mr. T]
<i>"1.78 billion won. 3.18 billion won."</i>

584
00:22:48,367 --> 00:22:49,869
<i>We were rolling in money.</i>

585
00:22:50,452 --> 00:22:52,829
MR. T APPROVED AND MANAGED THE FUNDS

586
00:22:52,830 --> 00:22:54,831
- Whose is it?
- [Mr. T] It's mine.

587
00:22:54,832 --> 00:22:57,542
- You mean it's ours.
- [Mr. T] Right, that's what I meant.

588
00:22:57,543 --> 00:22:58,668
SETTING HIM STRAIGHT

589
00:22:58,669 --> 00:23:01,088
What evidence did you bring, Mr. P?

590
00:23:01,714 --> 00:23:02,882
[Mr. P] I found this.

591
00:23:05,968 --> 00:23:07,260
Hey, this is pretty major.

592
00:23:07,261 --> 00:23:08,970
- You think so? I thought--
- Hang on.

593
00:23:08,971 --> 00:23:11,097
- I'm a bit clueless.
- Because this log is just

594
00:23:11,098 --> 00:23:13,600
the ledger of the money
we made from our scam.

595
00:23:13,601 --> 00:23:15,560
- That's all it is. You need to see this.
- Okay.

596
00:23:15,561 --> 00:23:16,769
This is what really matters.

597
00:23:16,770 --> 00:23:18,147
- Bullet marks.
- Where is this?

598
00:23:18,731 --> 00:23:20,566
Oh, they're mine!

599
00:23:21,233 --> 00:23:22,317
[Mr. T] What?

600
00:23:22,318 --> 00:23:23,526
I fired four bullets.

601
00:23:23,527 --> 00:23:25,528
I was so startled,
I fired four shots into the wall.

602
00:23:25,529 --> 00:23:27,322
EVERYTHING IS HIS

603
00:23:27,323 --> 00:23:29,824
He's so excited to see evidence
about the shots he fired.

604
00:23:29,825 --> 00:23:32,577
Didn't you hear four shots
at around 00:21?

605
00:23:32,578 --> 00:23:36,331
<i>I was startled by a sound,
so I shot in that direction.</i>

606
00:23:36,332 --> 00:23:38,082
But why did you shoot?

607
00:23:38,083 --> 00:23:40,960
I was on the move at the time,

608
00:23:40,961 --> 00:23:43,504
and I heard the sound
of someone chasing after me--

609
00:23:43,505 --> 00:23:45,256
- You weren't in the control tower?
- No.

610
00:23:45,257 --> 00:23:47,634
- I was at the far end.
- [Mr. L] Were you past the gate?

611
00:23:47,635 --> 00:23:50,930
At the outer walls. I was really startled.

612
00:23:51,430 --> 00:23:52,973
I only meant to shoot one bullet.

613
00:23:53,474 --> 00:23:55,017
I didn't know it'd keep firing.

614
00:24:01,982 --> 00:24:04,692
Did you have any bullets left over?

615
00:24:04,693 --> 00:24:06,487
- Yes.
- [Mr. L] You had more bullets.

616
00:24:06,987 --> 00:24:09,572
Someone look at this.
I can't figure it out.

617
00:24:09,573 --> 00:24:12,784
"A equals 17. B equals 22."

618
00:24:12,785 --> 00:24:14,035
What does this mean?

619
00:24:14,036 --> 00:24:15,411
- A equals 10?
- [Mr. P] It's 17.

620
00:24:15,412 --> 00:24:17,872
And B equals 22.

621
00:24:17,873 --> 00:24:20,625
- What?
- I don't know.

622
00:24:20,626 --> 00:24:22,585
I'm really bad at math.

623
00:24:22,586 --> 00:24:23,587
[Ms. H] What was A?

624
00:24:24,380 --> 00:24:26,130
A is 17 and B is 22.

625
00:24:26,131 --> 00:24:27,840
[Ms. H] A is 17 and B is 22?

626
00:24:27,841 --> 00:24:29,300
WHAT IS THIS MYSTERY EQUATION?

627
00:24:29,301 --> 00:24:31,970
Honestly, I'm totally clueless so far.

628
00:24:31,971 --> 00:24:34,430
It's hard to guess who it could be.

629
00:24:34,431 --> 00:24:37,392
Didn't this just prove

630
00:24:37,393 --> 00:24:39,019
that I never shot anyone?

631
00:24:39,603 --> 00:24:42,397
It proves you shot four bullets then,

632
00:24:42,398 --> 00:24:43,940
but shots were fired after that.

633
00:24:43,941 --> 00:24:45,066
[Mr. X] Yeah, around three more.

634
00:24:45,067 --> 00:24:47,319
And you had bullets left over
after those four shots.

635
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:50,488
But there was a shot fired before this.

636
00:24:50,489 --> 00:24:52,115
I think that was me.

637
00:24:52,116 --> 00:24:53,324
Initially…

638
00:24:53,325 --> 00:24:56,078
WHAT IS HE SAYING?

639
00:24:56,662 --> 00:24:58,162
- You fired shots?
- Yeah.

640
00:24:58,163 --> 00:25:01,207
But earlier, you said you just cuffed him.

641
00:25:01,208 --> 00:25:04,002
But we had a pretty big fight before that.

642
00:25:04,003 --> 00:25:07,130
- You used a gun on your brother?
- You didn't just throw fists?

643
00:25:07,131 --> 00:25:09,590
What kind of brothers shoot at each other?

644
00:25:09,591 --> 00:25:11,009
Did he get hit?

645
00:25:11,010 --> 00:25:12,844
He did.

646
00:25:12,845 --> 00:25:14,429
- What?
- [Ms. H] Where did he get hit?

647
00:25:14,430 --> 00:25:15,888
This doesn't make sense.

648
00:25:15,889 --> 00:25:17,974
You get into a fight
and aim at your brother?

649
00:25:17,975 --> 00:25:20,518
What kind of siblings fight with guns?

650
00:25:20,519 --> 00:25:21,811
Ones from a dysfunctional family.

651
00:25:21,812 --> 00:25:23,771
- [Mr. P] A total trainwreck.
- [Mr. X] Yeah.

652
00:25:23,772 --> 00:25:25,565
What time did you shoot him?

653
00:25:25,566 --> 00:25:27,650
I shot him at around 00:15.

654
00:25:27,651 --> 00:25:28,735
[Mr. P] You're a scary guy.

655
00:25:28,736 --> 00:25:31,863
FIRST GUNFIRE

656
00:25:31,864 --> 00:25:34,949
[host] <i>Starting Round 2,
the private conversation zones are open.</i>

657
00:25:34,950 --> 00:25:39,996
<i>Anyone who wants a private conversation
can freely access this zone.</i>

658
00:25:39,997 --> 00:25:42,415
I reckon we all want to talk to Mr. L.

659
00:25:42,416 --> 00:25:44,167
I'm up for it.

660
00:25:44,168 --> 00:25:47,253
Then, why don't we three talk first?

661
00:25:47,254 --> 00:25:48,629
Then, I'll talk to Bokgil. [chuckles]

662
00:25:48,630 --> 00:25:50,423
I'm not Bokgil.

663
00:25:50,424 --> 00:25:51,507
I'm Mr. P.

664
00:25:51,508 --> 00:25:54,052
We need to quickly find the traitor.

665
00:25:54,053 --> 00:25:55,970
You're the traitor,

666
00:25:55,971 --> 00:25:57,472
but you're acting innocent.

667
00:25:57,473 --> 00:25:59,140
I had no reason to kill my brother.

668
00:25:59,141 --> 00:26:00,767
Even though you shot him?

669
00:26:00,768 --> 00:26:03,102
That was because he shot me first.

670
00:26:03,103 --> 00:26:05,313
- The first gunfire was a single shot.
- [Mr. X] Yeah.

671
00:26:05,314 --> 00:26:07,523
[Mr. L] <i>The first shot in the timeline</i>

672
00:26:07,524 --> 00:26:10,068
<i>was the sound
of me and my brother fighting.</i>

673
00:26:10,069 --> 00:26:12,111
But then we should have heard two shots.

674
00:26:12,112 --> 00:26:13,738
But we both fired at the same time.

675
00:26:13,739 --> 00:26:15,782
So you would have just heard one shot.

676
00:26:15,783 --> 00:26:18,910
Then the clue we saw earlier,

677
00:26:18,911 --> 00:26:20,203
the AI-analysis report,

678
00:26:20,204 --> 00:26:23,581
<i>was about your fight with your brother.</i>

679
00:26:23,582 --> 00:26:27,335
Yeah, I shot my brother,
and it grazed his arm here.

680
00:26:27,336 --> 00:26:29,504
- [Mr. X] <i>It wasn't fatal?
- It wasn't life-threatening.</i>

681
00:26:29,505 --> 00:26:31,881
<i>I also got shot in the shoulder,
so it hurts a little.</i>

682
00:26:31,882 --> 00:26:32,965
Your shoulder too?

683
00:26:32,966 --> 00:26:34,634
My injury was worse than his.

684
00:26:34,635 --> 00:26:37,136
If you ever want to play e-sports
after you get out…

685
00:26:37,137 --> 00:26:38,596
[Mr. X] You're at a disadvantage.

686
00:26:38,597 --> 00:26:40,973
Who are you? Where are you from?

687
00:26:40,974 --> 00:26:42,058
- I am… [chuckles]
- [chuckles]

688
00:26:42,059 --> 00:26:43,476
Ms. H.

689
00:26:43,477 --> 00:26:45,603
- My name is H.
- Yeah.

690
00:26:45,604 --> 00:26:47,939
- I used to be an influencer.
- Right.

691
00:26:47,940 --> 00:26:49,857
I wanted to make big money.

692
00:26:49,858 --> 00:26:51,192
Same as me.

693
00:26:51,193 --> 00:26:54,070
[clicks tongue] I found out
through a friend

694
00:26:54,071 --> 00:26:55,614
- about the MASTER.
- A friend?

695
00:26:56,198 --> 00:27:01,244
SHE HEARD ABOUT THE MASTER
AT A SOCIAL GATHERING OF ELITES

696
00:27:01,245 --> 00:27:03,329
- I need the MASTER.
- [Mr. P grunting]

697
00:27:03,330 --> 00:27:05,706
I wanted to use him to get filthy rich.

698
00:27:05,707 --> 00:27:07,083
Me too! We're the same.

699
00:27:07,084 --> 00:27:08,167
[Ms. H] Yeah.

700
00:27:08,168 --> 00:27:10,628
- I needed him to stay alive.
- [Mr. P] Same.

701
00:27:10,629 --> 00:27:12,380
I was so shocked to find out he was dead.

702
00:27:12,381 --> 00:27:14,382
My question for you is,

703
00:27:14,383 --> 00:27:16,551
you had a gun, right?

704
00:27:16,552 --> 00:27:17,635
Of course.

705
00:27:17,636 --> 00:27:18,886
When did you use it?

706
00:27:18,887 --> 00:27:20,471
- I didn't.
- You didn't use it?

707
00:27:20,472 --> 00:27:21,889
- Not at all?
- Never.

708
00:27:21,890 --> 00:27:24,475
If I find evidence that you're lying,
you're the culprit.

709
00:27:24,476 --> 00:27:25,726
I never fired, I tell you.

710
00:27:25,727 --> 00:27:27,395
- So you never used it.
- Right.

711
00:27:27,396 --> 00:27:28,604
Gun? No way.

712
00:27:28,605 --> 00:27:29,939
- [Ms. H] I--
- Did you use yours?

713
00:27:29,940 --> 00:27:31,023
[Ms. H] Well, I…

714
00:27:31,024 --> 00:27:33,067
Let's talk about you.

715
00:27:33,068 --> 00:27:34,945
- [chuckling] I don't know--
- I'll do the note-taking.

716
00:27:35,529 --> 00:27:38,281
- Hey, this is my confidential file.
- [Mr. P chuckling]

717
00:27:38,282 --> 00:27:40,741
[Ms. H] Write your notes here. Gosh.

718
00:27:40,742 --> 00:27:43,911
If we escaped with the MASTER,
we could get filthy rich, right?

719
00:27:43,912 --> 00:27:47,081
So I was making my way
to the MASTER when I heard a shot.

720
00:27:47,082 --> 00:27:49,209
So I fired a bullet by accident.

721
00:27:50,085 --> 00:27:51,670
I pulled the trigger
because I was so scared.

722
00:27:52,379 --> 00:27:55,089
- To be honest, I also fired a shot.
- What?

723
00:27:55,090 --> 00:27:56,215
You liar!

724
00:27:56,216 --> 00:27:58,801
But I didn't shoot at a person.
I shot a cabinet.

725
00:27:58,802 --> 00:28:01,721
I kept another item stored in a cabinet.

726
00:28:01,722 --> 00:28:03,014
- I used my gun…
- Yeah?

727
00:28:03,015 --> 00:28:05,850
…to get that item
and pass it on to the MASTER.

728
00:28:05,851 --> 00:28:07,560
- To open the cabinet?
- That's right.

729
00:28:07,561 --> 00:28:08,770
I didn't know the passcode.

730
00:28:09,354 --> 00:28:12,481
HE USED HIS GUN TO OPEN THE CABINET?

731
00:28:12,482 --> 00:28:14,567
Why did you leave
after shooting your brother?

732
00:28:14,568 --> 00:28:18,195
I went to the storage room
to find a hard disk.

733
00:28:18,196 --> 00:28:19,740
Why did you need to find a hard disk?

734
00:28:20,532 --> 00:28:23,243
THE HARD DISK CONTAINS KEY DATA
ABOUT THEIR ORGANIZATION

735
00:28:23,827 --> 00:28:27,580
HE HID A BACKUP HARD DISK OF THE SERVER
IN THE STORAGE ROOM BEFORE IMPRISONMENT

736
00:28:27,581 --> 00:28:29,665
There's a hard disk in the storage room,

737
00:28:29,666 --> 00:28:32,043
and we can't let the police
get their hands on it.

738
00:28:32,044 --> 00:28:34,254
Oh, you wanted to destroy the evidence?

739
00:28:34,838 --> 00:28:39,383
HE WENT FROM THE CONTROL TOWER
TO THE STORAGE ROOM AT 00:19

740
00:28:39,384 --> 00:28:41,719
Can you share your alibis,
from the beginning?

741
00:28:41,720 --> 00:28:45,014
I need to know where you were
at 00:21, 00:25, and 00:30.

742
00:28:45,015 --> 00:28:48,684
THE CHARACTER GUIDES SHOW
THE TIMELINE OF THEIR MOVEMENTS

743
00:28:48,685 --> 00:28:51,062
For ten minutes,

744
00:28:51,063 --> 00:28:52,605
I had no idea what to do,
so I just waited.

745
00:28:52,606 --> 00:28:54,398
- I was just too anxious.
- Right.

746
00:28:54,399 --> 00:28:58,236
Since it's such a complex labyrinth,
I thought circling the perimeter was best.

747
00:28:58,820 --> 00:29:00,821
MR. X MOVED AROUND THE OUTSIDE
TO GET HIS BEARINGS

748
00:29:00,822 --> 00:29:02,782
WORKSHOP 2, DINING HALL 2, SHOWERS

749
00:29:02,783 --> 00:29:05,993
Staying by the perimeter,
I went from the showers to Workshop 1.

750
00:29:05,994 --> 00:29:07,078
[Mr. T] <i>The showers?</i>

751
00:29:07,079 --> 00:29:09,581
[Mr. X] <i>I passed the showers
and Workshop 1.</i>

752
00:29:10,165 --> 00:29:13,334
And that's where I heard
a strange sound and fired.

753
00:29:13,335 --> 00:29:16,463
- By Workshop 1?
- Yeah. It was 00:21 at the time.

754
00:29:17,381 --> 00:29:19,799
HE HEARD SOMEONE COMING
AND FIRED FOUR BULLETS

755
00:29:19,800 --> 00:29:21,300
After that,

756
00:29:21,301 --> 00:29:23,637
I passed Gate 3
to get to the control tower.

757
00:29:24,221 --> 00:29:25,304
What time was that?

758
00:29:25,305 --> 00:29:27,557
- It was around 00:35.
- [grunts]

759
00:29:28,642 --> 00:29:31,352
HE ARRIVED AT THE CONTROL TOWER
AT AROUND 00:35

760
00:29:31,353 --> 00:29:33,813
- [Mr. X] Mr. T.
- I set out from the green zone,

761
00:29:33,814 --> 00:29:37,066
<i>and at around 00:10,
I found that Gate 1 was locked,</i>

762
00:29:37,067 --> 00:29:39,193
<i>so I went through the showers to Gate 2.</i>

763
00:29:39,194 --> 00:29:41,779
I got to Gate 2 through the showers,
but it wouldn't open.

764
00:29:41,780 --> 00:29:43,948
So I got a bit anxious at that point.

765
00:29:43,949 --> 00:29:47,118
<i>In the corridor between Gate 2
and the showers,</i>

766
00:29:47,119 --> 00:29:49,954
<i>I stood around for a bit
not knowing what to do.</i>

767
00:29:49,955 --> 00:29:52,332
- My mind went blank.
- [Mr. L] You zoned out?

768
00:29:52,916 --> 00:29:54,542
HE ZONED OUT?

769
00:29:54,543 --> 00:29:56,377
What were you doing at 00:25?

770
00:29:56,378 --> 00:29:58,170
Yeah. What did you at 00:25?

771
00:29:58,171 --> 00:29:59,797
Oh, that's when I zoned out.

772
00:29:59,798 --> 00:30:01,841
- Until 00:25?
- [Mr. L] What about at 00:30?

773
00:30:01,842 --> 00:30:03,176
It doesn't say what I did at 00:25.

774
00:30:03,927 --> 00:30:06,178
- At 00:30, at Gate 3--
- It doesn't say?

775
00:30:06,179 --> 00:30:07,346
No, at 00:30,

776
00:30:07,347 --> 00:30:09,474
I learned Gate 3 was open,
so I went there.

777
00:30:10,100 --> 00:30:11,308
Got it.

778
00:30:11,309 --> 00:30:13,102
Okay. You don't remember?

779
00:30:13,103 --> 00:30:14,979
- It really doesn't say!
- You don't know your alibi.

780
00:30:14,980 --> 00:30:16,063
Got it.

781
00:30:16,064 --> 00:30:19,358
Mr. T's alibi is a bit suspicious.

782
00:30:19,359 --> 00:30:20,568
He wouldn't tell us.

783
00:30:20,569 --> 00:30:22,987
He's definitely hiding something.

784
00:30:22,988 --> 00:30:24,655
ROUND 2 CARD CLUES
CRUMPLED NOTE, SEARCH HISTORY

785
00:30:24,656 --> 00:30:26,824
FIREARM INSPECTION LOG,
H'S FRIEND'S TESTIMONY, CCTV AUDIO RECORD

786
00:30:26,825 --> 00:30:29,702
[host] <i>Here is the next shared clue.</i>

787
00:30:29,703 --> 00:30:31,579
[Mr. X] You can tell
from the number of bullets.

788
00:30:31,580 --> 00:30:32,748
[Ms. H] We all fired at least once.

789
00:30:33,331 --> 00:30:34,958
5 OUT OF 6 BULLETS REMAINING

790
00:30:35,542 --> 00:30:36,960
5 OUT OF 6 BULLETS REMAINING

791
00:30:37,544 --> 00:30:39,086
2 OUT OF 6 BULLETS REMAINING

792
00:30:39,087 --> 00:30:40,756
Holding the trigger down
doesn't release a bullet.

793
00:30:41,339 --> 00:30:42,631
5 OUT OF 6 BULLETS REMAINING

794
00:30:42,632 --> 00:30:44,842
[host] <i>Please exchange card clues.</i>

795
00:30:44,843 --> 00:30:46,510
- Go ahead.
- [Ms. H] I'll take one from Mr. P.

796
00:30:46,511 --> 00:30:48,804
Why me? You didn't suspect me earlier.

797
00:30:48,805 --> 00:30:50,057
Hang on. I'll take this one.

798
00:30:50,682 --> 00:30:52,224
MR. P TAKES ONE FROM MR. L

799
00:30:52,225 --> 00:30:53,810
Mr. P has many cards left,

800
00:30:54,644 --> 00:30:55,770
so I'll take one from him.

801
00:30:55,771 --> 00:30:57,104
I'll take one from Ms. H.

802
00:30:57,105 --> 00:30:59,149
I'm also starting to wonder about Ms. H.

803
00:31:04,654 --> 00:31:05,739
I got something heavy.

804
00:31:06,323 --> 00:31:07,491
What's this?

805
00:31:08,200 --> 00:31:09,326
[Mr. L] The letter "O."

806
00:31:13,705 --> 00:31:16,082
ROUND 3

807
00:31:16,083 --> 00:31:19,126
Gosh. Mr. L, you are rich.

808
00:31:19,127 --> 00:31:22,463
<i>This looks like a ledger
showing the profit shares</i>

809
00:31:22,464 --> 00:31:25,467
<i>of Mr. L and the MASTER.</i>

810
00:31:26,259 --> 00:31:28,677
- He did it for the money.
- [Mr. T gasps] He took his cut.

811
00:31:28,678 --> 00:31:30,346
You killed your brother over money.

812
00:31:30,347 --> 00:31:33,432
I also have a lot of evidence
related to finances.

813
00:31:33,433 --> 00:31:36,310
- I have Eom Jihoon's account details.
- [all gasping]

814
00:31:36,311 --> 00:31:37,645
- Your late brother.
- Yeah.

815
00:31:37,646 --> 00:31:39,355
- [Mr. L] He was your brother, right?
- Yes.

816
00:31:39,356 --> 00:31:42,525
[Mr. L] <i>He wired the MASTER
three installments of 50 million won.</i>

817
00:31:42,526 --> 00:31:44,401
- Three installments.
- [Mr. T] Who did?

818
00:31:44,402 --> 00:31:45,820
- My brother.
- [Mr. T] Your brother?

819
00:31:45,821 --> 00:31:48,656
- Was he a victim of the scam?
- [Mr. X] He must have been.

820
00:31:48,657 --> 00:31:50,366
He killed him over 50 million won?

821
00:31:50,367 --> 00:31:51,659
- 150 million won.
- Oh, 150 million?

822
00:31:51,660 --> 00:31:55,121
He was scammed three times,
so it was 150 million.

823
00:31:55,122 --> 00:31:59,375
This means Ms. H
had a clear motive to kill the MASTER.

824
00:31:59,376 --> 00:32:02,086
Eom Jihoon is actually my ex-husband.

825
00:32:02,087 --> 00:32:04,880
I killed him. [chuckling]

826
00:32:04,881 --> 00:32:07,341
They all just assumed he was my brother.

827
00:32:07,342 --> 00:32:10,928
"Right! My brother!"
and I went along with it.

828
00:32:10,929 --> 00:32:14,641
I found out through a friend
about the MASTER.

829
00:32:15,809 --> 00:32:18,687
SHE THOUGHT HER HUSBAND WAS RICH

830
00:32:19,271 --> 00:32:21,356
HE WAS THE FRONT MAN
OF THE MONEY-LAUNDERING COMPANY "PANTS"

831
00:32:21,940 --> 00:32:24,818
THE MASTER WAS SOMEONE ELSE

832
00:32:25,402 --> 00:32:28,321
SHE CHOSE TO DITCH HER HUSBAND

833
00:32:28,905 --> 00:32:31,283
AND BEGAN TO OBSESS OVER
GAINING REAL POWER

834
00:32:31,867 --> 00:32:33,701
WHAT WAS AMBITIOUS MS. H'S PLAN?

835
00:32:33,702 --> 00:32:37,329
But I had no idea my brother
wired the MASTER money.

836
00:32:37,330 --> 00:32:38,831
Are you sure about that?

837
00:32:38,832 --> 00:32:41,167
How can we believe that?

838
00:32:41,168 --> 00:32:43,836
Of course, I could be lying.
But one thing's for sure.

839
00:32:43,837 --> 00:32:47,256
I stood to gain a lot from meeting
the MASTER on the outside,

840
00:32:47,257 --> 00:32:49,717
so I could never kill him.

841
00:32:49,718 --> 00:32:52,011
You weren't trying to get out
of the organization by killing him?

842
00:32:52,012 --> 00:32:55,264
No, I wasn't.
I'm so shocked by this evidence.

843
00:32:55,265 --> 00:32:57,641
But that doesn't add up

844
00:32:57,642 --> 00:33:00,477
because didn't you read
an upsetting memo saying…

845
00:33:00,478 --> 00:33:02,813
- Me?
-…you'd be sorted out after the escape?

846
00:33:02,814 --> 00:33:04,440
She would be sorted out after escaping?

847
00:33:04,441 --> 00:33:08,569
- Yeah, didn't you see that memo?
- I did.

848
00:33:08,570 --> 00:33:10,572
Doesn't that mean
you were going to be killed?

849
00:33:11,698 --> 00:33:15,409
No, it meant we'd wipe the slate clean
and start afresh together

850
00:33:15,410 --> 00:33:16,911
so we could stand tall.

851
00:33:16,912 --> 00:33:19,164
No, that's spinning it too positively.

852
00:33:19,748 --> 00:33:23,667
DID THE MASTER PLAN TO KILL MS. H?

853
00:33:23,668 --> 00:33:26,378
[host] <i>Starting Round 3,
the CCTV zone is open.</i>

854
00:33:26,379 --> 00:33:30,175
<i>Use the password cards
to access the security footage.</i>

855
00:33:30,759 --> 00:33:32,636
CCTV ZONE

856
00:33:33,220 --> 00:33:36,222
CCTV PASSWORD ON BONUS CARD
FOUND NEXT TO OBJECT CLUE

857
00:33:36,223 --> 00:33:37,431
I'll check out the CCTV.

858
00:33:37,432 --> 00:33:39,850
- Can I go with you?
- [Mr. T] Of course.

859
00:33:39,851 --> 00:33:41,268
RUSHING OVER

860
00:33:41,269 --> 00:33:43,604
[Mr. X] I see you can still run
with that leg.

861
00:33:43,605 --> 00:33:45,272
- The pain's eased up again.
- [Mr. X chuckles]

862
00:33:45,273 --> 00:33:46,398
[exclaims]

863
00:33:46,399 --> 00:33:47,400
- What's this?
- Who's that?

864
00:33:48,360 --> 00:33:50,236
- [Mr. X] "I will shoot."
- [Mr. L] Gate 4.

865
00:33:50,237 --> 00:33:52,196
"I will shoot you!"

866
00:33:52,197 --> 00:33:54,490
- [Ms. H] Is that me?
- [Mr. L] Who is that?

867
00:33:54,491 --> 00:33:56,325
SHE LOWERED HER GUN WITHOUT SHOOTING

868
00:33:56,326 --> 00:33:59,036
[Mr. X] Two shots were fired at 00:31.

869
00:33:59,037 --> 00:34:00,579
But she decided not to shoot.

870
00:34:00,580 --> 00:34:02,581
- She didn't shoot?
- [exclaims]

871
00:34:02,582 --> 00:34:04,500
- She didn't shoot.
- [Mr. X] It seems not.

872
00:34:04,501 --> 00:34:05,961
MORE CCTV FOOTAGE

873
00:34:07,545 --> 00:34:08,629
- What's that?
- [exclaims]

874
00:34:08,630 --> 00:34:10,047
[gasps]

875
00:34:10,048 --> 00:34:12,050
[♪ dramatic music playing]

876
00:34:14,427 --> 00:34:15,428
Oh, my.

877
00:34:16,846 --> 00:34:18,305
I told you earlier, didn't I?

878
00:34:18,306 --> 00:34:19,765
That's how you opened the cabinet?

879
00:34:19,766 --> 00:34:22,352
I'm cleared! I'm in the clear.

880
00:34:22,936 --> 00:34:25,562
HE SHOT THE CABINET TO OPEN IT

881
00:34:25,563 --> 00:34:27,274
- What was inside it?
- What was in there?

882
00:34:29,442 --> 00:34:32,027
WHAT DID HE PULL OUT OF THE CABINET?

883
00:34:32,028 --> 00:34:34,488
My cover was almost blown,

884
00:34:34,489 --> 00:34:36,448
but they haven't caught on.

885
00:34:36,449 --> 00:34:39,702
<i>I'm actually an undercover cop.</i>

886
00:34:39,703 --> 00:34:42,705
<i>I shot the cabinet to get
the hidden investigation records.</i>

887
00:34:42,706 --> 00:34:45,582
But since it's such a big murder case,

888
00:34:45,583 --> 00:34:48,294
that evidence has taken a back seat.
It's a relief.

889
00:34:48,295 --> 00:34:49,503
MR. P IS AN UNDERCOVER COP

890
00:34:49,504 --> 00:34:50,964
- I'll enter my password now.
- Sure.

891
00:34:52,841 --> 00:34:54,175
Who's that?

892
00:34:59,306 --> 00:35:00,472
- Who is that?
- Who's that?

893
00:35:00,473 --> 00:35:02,349
- Who is it?
- Who is that?

894
00:35:02,350 --> 00:35:04,977
- You said you went to the storage room.
- I did.

895
00:35:04,978 --> 00:35:06,687
You went to get the hard disk.

896
00:35:06,688 --> 00:35:10,441
I went to the storage room
to find a hard disk.

897
00:35:10,442 --> 00:35:11,942
But I failed to find the disk.

898
00:35:11,943 --> 00:35:14,195
- I think that's what you're seeing.
- [Ms. H] What?

899
00:35:19,159 --> 00:35:21,577
MR. L TRIED TO RETURN
TO THE CONTROL TOWER AT 00:29

900
00:35:21,578 --> 00:35:23,120
I have one more.

901
00:35:23,121 --> 00:35:24,913
[♪ intriguing music playing]

902
00:35:24,914 --> 00:35:27,500
- [Mr. P] You… Hang on.
- The hard disk.

903
00:35:28,501 --> 00:35:34,256
MS. H TOOK SOMETHING OUT
OF THE STORAGE ROOM AT 00:10

904
00:35:34,257 --> 00:35:35,841
It's the hard disk.

905
00:35:35,842 --> 00:35:37,551
- She took it.
- Earlier…

906
00:35:37,552 --> 00:35:39,303
I took that hard drive.

907
00:35:39,304 --> 00:35:40,638
- Why?
- How'd you know about it?

908
00:35:40,639 --> 00:35:42,891
We need that disk to work with the MASTER.

909
00:35:43,475 --> 00:35:44,684
[Mr. P] To get filthy rich.

910
00:35:45,685 --> 00:35:47,354
THE FINAL CCTV CLUE

911
00:35:48,396 --> 00:35:50,314
- Who's that?
- Oh, I know.

912
00:35:50,315 --> 00:35:51,815
- [Ms. H] <i>He's limping.</i>
- [Mr. P] <i>Right.</i>

913
00:35:51,816 --> 00:35:54,276
MR. L ENTERED THE CONTROL TOWER

914
00:35:54,277 --> 00:35:55,278
What's he doing?

915
00:35:58,156 --> 00:35:59,865
<i>-He suddenly stopped limping!
- What the heck?</i>

916
00:35:59,866 --> 00:36:01,950
- He suddenly stopped limping!
- [Mr. T] What the heck?

917
00:36:01,951 --> 00:36:04,079
Whoa, that's chilling!

918
00:36:04,663 --> 00:36:06,539
HE WAS LIMPING WHEN HE WENT IN

919
00:36:07,457 --> 00:36:10,210
BUT HE WASN'T LIMPING 10 MINUTES LATER

920
00:36:12,962 --> 00:36:15,089
Sometimes you're limping,
sometimes you're not.

921
00:36:15,090 --> 00:36:16,924
What a crazy twist.

922
00:36:16,925 --> 00:36:18,509
Oh!

923
00:36:18,510 --> 00:36:22,055
You're the MASTER.

924
00:36:48,331 --> 00:36:50,333
Translation by Hannah Og Nam
